ctor-image)\n\n[Srinibash Bartia](https://in.linkedin.com/in/srinibash-bartia-39613b29b?trk=public_post_comment_actor-name)\nProduct design | UX Designer | Tech, Ecommerce and Saas\n\n 7mo \n\n*   [Report this comment](https://www.linkedin.com/uas/login?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_ellipsis-menu-semaphore-sign-in-redirect&guestReportContentType=COMMENT&_f=guest-reporting)\n\nVery insightful. One key takeaway I would like to add is the importance of understanding the user's needs and desires. It's not enough to simply create a visually appealing product, it must also serve a purpose and solve a problem for the user. Additionally, continuously gathering feedback and making improvements based on that feedback is crucial for perfecting the design. As Jobs said, Design is not just what it looks like and feels like. Design is how it works.\"\n\n[Like](https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Elinkedin%2Ecom%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_like)[Reply](https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Elinkedin%2Ecom%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_reply)[2 Reactions](https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Elinkedin%2Ecom%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_reactions) 3 Reactions \n\n[![Image 15: Bhavya T., graphic](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/felixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh)](https://ca.linkedin.com/in/bhavya-t-67a630121?trk=public_post_comment_actor-image)\n\n[Bhavya T.](https://ca.linkedin.com/in/bhavya-t-67a630121?trk=public_post_comment_actor-name)\nProduct Designer | Specialized in Fintech, SaaS, AI-Web Applications B2B, B2B2C | 0→1 & 1→2 Scalable Products\n\n 7mo \n\n*   [Report this comment](https://www.linkedin.com/uas/login?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_ellipsis-menu-semaphore-sign-in-redirect&guestReportContentType=COMMENT&_f=guest-reporting)\n\nAs a Product Designer, I’ve always admired Steve Jobs’ vision, but after 2022, the relationship between people and technology has fundamentally changed. Back then, he said, ‘If users need a manual, the design has failed.’ Today, design requires mindful guidance — not to simply make things easy, but to ensure users retain control and active engagement. • Then: Products were tools we fully controlled. • Now: With AI, control is shared, often reducing our own cognitive engagement and habit of critical thinking. Jobs also emphasized, ‘Don’t rely on market research; show people the future.’ But now, we have to consider how AI influences user behavior over time. As reliance on AI grows, there’s a risk that users may lose essential skills or even the habit of independent thought. Our designs must support users, not replace their cognitive involvement. In this new era, design isn’t just about simplicity or innovation. It’s about fostering long-term user growth and engagement, ensuring our products don’t diminish the very abilities that make us human.🫠\n\n[Like](https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Elinkedin%2Ecom%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_post_comment_like)[Reply](https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Elinkedin%2Ecom%2Fposts%2Ffelixleezd_steve-jobs-on-how-to-perfect-product-design-activity-7259573425705254912-hYCh&trk=public_